# Example 55: OCSP Stapling at the Proxy. (co-10)
#
# When a browser gets your cert, it may phone the CA (OCSP) to check the cert
# has not been revoked -- a slow, privacy-leaking extra round trip. OCSP
# STAPLING makes the PROXY fetch that "still valid" proof and attach ("staple")
# it to the handshake, so the browser trusts the cert without calling the CA.
# Caddy does this by default; here is how to confirm it, and the Nginx knobs.

# --- Caddy: OCSP stapling is ON by default -- just VERIFY it ------------------
# (No Caddyfile change needed; this block documents the verification.)
# Test with openssl: a 'OCSP Response Status: successful' line means it is stapled.
#   echo | openssl s_client -connect myapp.example.com:443 -status 2>/dev/null \
#     | grep -A2 'OCSP Response Status'
# => expect: 'OCSP Response Status: successful'

# --- Nginx: turn it ON explicitly (if using Nginx, Example 52) ----------------
# Inside the 'server { listen 443 ssl; ... }' block:
#   ssl_stapling on;                         # => enable OCSP stapling
#   ssl_stapling_verify on;                  # => verify the CA's response before stapling
#   resolver 1.1.1.1 valid=300s;            # => how Nginx reaches the OCSP responder
#   resolver_timeout 5s;                     # => do not block the handshake on a slow resolver

# --- Why it matters (co-10) --------------------------------------------------
# Without stapling, every new client MAY make an extra call to the CA -- adding
# latency and leaking (to the CA) which site the client visited. Stapling moves
# that call to the proxy, done once and reused, for a faster, more private TLS.
# [verify] 'OCSP Response Status: successful' from the openssl probe (co-10 proof).